On 2008-12-18, Erwin M. <erwin71m@gmail.com> wrote:
> The crapshoot is that the situation where prayer is most helpful --
> the divine full heal -- is under circumstances that would likely prove
> fatal if you're wrong about the state of your prayer timeout. 

Indeed.  But then, it's easy to be right about it.

http://nethack.wikia.com/wiki/Image:Rnz100.svg

This is a graph of rnz(100)'s probability distribution.

There are two important parts to this graph.  The first part is that
by and large, the vast majority of occurrences are below or equal to
100.  This means that most of the time, your prayer timeout will be 350
turns or less.  The most probable outcome is in fact a number _half_
that, or 175 turns.

This isn't good enough if you're in "about to die" circumstances, of
course.  You need to know with some degree of higher confidence.  So we
examine the next important part of the graph, which is the precipitous
drop-off at twice the original value; in other words, it is incredibly
unlikely that your prayer timeout will ever be more than 700 turns.  At
that point, the risk of attempting to pray is minor compared to the
risks of not praying; after all, you're considering prayer in the first
place, aren't you?

So it's very simple: inscribe (item) with the turn number of your last
successful prayer.  700 turns later, you can be sufficiently confident
of praying again; 350 turns later, it's worth a try in order to fix some
given trouble (as long as you aren't about to die on a failed prayer).

It is discussed in greater depth at:

http://nethack.wikia.com/wiki/Rnz

The "prayer timeout" page on wikia notes that 1058 is the 95% confidence
interval.  Given the circumstances under which you're likely to be
praying, this must be measured against the probability that you're going
to die _without_ praying -- usually more than 5%.  I recommend starting
from 700 if you've never tried this before, and going down from there.

There are also circumstances which will decrease the timeout; if you are
in what the game defines as "major trouble", you get an effective -200
to your timeout.  If you are in "minor trouble", you get an effective
-100.  It helps to be aware of both of these, as well.

